(expand - the vas front end as a command, for the expansion check and the
 refusal tests. It preprocesses one assembly source with vas and writes
 the expansion, one statement per line, to the output path. On an error
 it writes vas's messages to standard error instead and exits with the
 number of errors, at most 255.

 Usage: expand <source .asm> <coreasm directory> <output path> [<definitions>]

 The definitions are names to define first, as nasm's -D defines them:
 one argument of words separated by spaces, each NAME or NAME=VALUE.)

see "../../../tools/vas/vas.vox".

(Each global here has a name that no function in vas.vox gives a variable
 of another type. Vox refuses a function's variable whose type differs
 from a global declared above the function, where the manual says the
 variable shadows the global: a Vox bug, reported, not yet numbered.
 vas.vox is seen above every global, so the bug cannot reach it, and the
 names are kept apart so that it never will.)

(A number after at borrows the descriptor the process already has rather
 than opening a path, so there is nothing here that can fail.)
Open a file for writing called 'standard error' at 2.

a list called 'the command arguments' is [].
append each 'command argument' from arguments's all to 'the command arguments'.
a number called 'how many were given' is 'the command arguments''s length.
a number called 'the fewest arguments' is 3.
a number called 'the most arguments' is 4.

If 'how many were given' is less than 'the fewest arguments' or 'how many were given' is greater than 'the most arguments' then,
    Write "usage: expand <source .asm> <coreasm directory> <output path> [<definitions>]\n" to 'standard error',
    Exit 2.

a text called 'the source to expand' is element 1 of 'the command arguments'.
a text called 'the coreasm to use' is element 2 of 'the command arguments'.
a text called 'the output to write' is element 3 of 'the command arguments'.
a text called 'the names to define' is "".
If 'how many were given' is 'the most arguments' then, set 'the names to define' to element 4 of 'the command arguments'.

a number called 'the error count' is 'expand file with definitions' of 'the source to expand' and 'the coreasm to use' and 'the output to write' and 'the names to define'.
If 'the error count' is greater than 0 then,
    a text called 'the messages to show' is 'assembler diagnostics' of 'the output to write',
    Write 'the messages to show' to 'standard error'.

(An exit status holds 0 to 255, so a larger count is given as 255, the
 most it can say.)
a number called 'the highest exit status' is 255.
If 'the error count' is greater than 'the highest exit status' then, set 'the error count' to 'the highest exit status'.
Exit 'the error count'.
